  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nSeabreeze convection is possible today due to an increase in \nmoisture and a weakness in the seemingly ever-present ridge. \n\nAbove normal temperatures will continue through Wednesday under high \npressure. Heat index values could reach 110 for a few hours across \nthe Coastal Plains and along the coast Wednesday, which could lead \nto a Heat Advisory. \n\nThings are going to really change later this week as a mid to upper \nlevel trough/low moves over Texas, increasing moisture across South \nTexas. An associated cold front will move into North Texas \nWednesday, while a pre-frontal trough develops across South Texas \nleading to showers and thunderstorms beginning Thursday. This \nmoisture will interact with the pre-frontal surface trough, leading \nto showers and thunderstorms beginning Thursday. The main cold front \nis forecast to move into South Texas Friday or Saturday, continuing \nrain chances as the attendant low slowly tracks across Texas. With \nPWATs nearing 2.5 inches, heavy rainfall is expected, with flash \nflooding possible. WPC has included most of South Texas in a Slight \nRisk of Excessive Rainfall Thursday and Friday. A Flash Flood Watch \nmay become necessary, but while confidence is high in the abundant \nmoisture, differences in model solutions keep confidence in the cold \nfront low so will continue to hold off on issuing this for now. \n\n\nMinor coastal flooding will be possible starting Tuesday as winds \nstrengthen across the gulf waters. The P-ETSS indicates tide levels \nup to 1.8 feet above MSL for Tuesday-Thursday, increasing to nearly \n2 feet by Friday during times of high tide. However, confidence is \nlow that water will reach the dunes due to swell periods of 7 \nseconds or less. In addition, the wind direction will play a role in \nhow high tides get. If they are more southerly, then higher tides \nare less likely, but east to southeast winds would be more \nconducive. Will continue to monitor trends for any changes.",
